# Rank 1084 / CVE-2019-9837: doorkeeper-openid_connect built its error response
# from the request's own redirect_uri and then redirected to it, so an attacker
# who could trigger the error chose where the browser went (CWE-601).
# lib/doorkeeper/openid_connect/helpers/controller.rb had
#
#   error = ::Doorkeeper::OAuth::ErrorResponse.new(name: exception.error_name, state: params[:state], redirect_uri: params[:redirect_uri])
#   ...
#   render json: error.body, status: :found, location: error.redirect_uri
#
# The fix 0267515452 uses params[:redirect_uri] only when pre_auth.valid?, which
# is where Doorkeeper checks the URI against the registered client, and otherwise
# takes pre_auth.error_response, which does not redirect anywhere the request
# asked for.
#
# ATTENTION, and the diagnosis separates cleanly into two findings.
#
#  - A sink anchored at args[N] does not see a value passed as a keyword
#    argument. The shipped Ruby binding for redirect_to emits
#    code.RedirectTargetSink at args[0], so redirect_to params[:redirect_uri]
#    reports VYQL-RF-002 and redirect_to location: params[:redirect_uri] reports
#    nothing, though the callee pattern matches both. Re-anchoring the same
#    binding at call makes the keyword form report. Keyword arguments are how
#    most Rails calls are written, so this is not specific to redirects.
#  - Rails render with a location: keyword is a redirect and is a sink nowhere.
#    Ruby binds redirect_to and redirect, both at args[0], and this code redirects
#    through render, which no binding names.
#
# Binding render location: would be generic -- Rails documents :location as
# setting the Location header -- and would still not make this rank CAUGHT,
# because the patched code makes the same call with the same tainted value in
# its valid? branch. The discriminator is pre_auth.valid?, Doorkeeper's own
# check of the URI against the registered client, and no concept describes a
# redirect target validated against a registered client, so both revisions would
# report. That is why nothing shipped here.
#
# Scanning the vulnerable helper reports 2 findings, both VYQL-INJ-003 on
# instance_exec at lines 58 and 73 with params[:max_age] and params[:prompt] as
# sources; neither is this CVE and neither survives reading, since the max_age
# value has .to_i applied and the block being executed is a configuration proc.
# The redirect at line 27 is not reported.
#
# verify_current_expected: attention-engine-gap

test "rank1084: the error response redirects to the request's own uri"
  lang ruby
  file lib/doorkeeper/openid_connect/helpers/controller.rb
  reject VYQL-RF-002
  code
  ```
  module Doorkeeper
    module OpenidConnect
      module Helpers
        module Controller
          def authenticate_resource_owner!
            super
          rescue Errors::OpenidConnectError => exception
            self.response_body = nil
            @_response_body = nil

            error = ::Doorkeeper::OAuth::ErrorResponse.new(name: exception.error_name, state: params[:state], redirect_uri: params[:redirect_uri])
            response.headers.merge!(error.headers)

            if error.redirectable?
              render json: error.body, status: :found, location: error.redirect_uri
            else
              render json: error.body, status: error.status
            end
          end
        end
      end
    end
  end
  ```

test "rank1084: the request uri is used only once the pre-auth validated it"
  lang ruby
  file lib/doorkeeper/openid_connect/helpers/controller.rb
  reject VYQL-RF-002
  code
  ```
  module Doorkeeper
    module OpenidConnect
      module Helpers
        module Controller
          def authenticate_resource_owner!
            super
          rescue Errors::OpenidConnectError => exception
            self.response_body = nil
            @_response_body = nil

            error_response = if pre_auth.valid?
              ::Doorkeeper::OAuth::ErrorResponse.new(
                name: exception.error_name,
                state: params[:state],
                redirect_uri: params[:redirect_uri]
              )
            else
              pre_auth.error_response
            end

            response.headers.merge!(error_response.headers)

            if error_response.redirectable?
              render json: error_response.body, status: :found, location: error_response.redirect_uri
            else
              render json: error_response.body, status: error_response.status
            end
          end
        end
      end
    end
  end
  ```

# The keyword-argument pair. Same callee, same tainted value, same shipped
# binding; only whether the argument is positional or keyed differs, and that
# is the difference between a report and silence.

test "rank1084: a redirect target passed as a positional argument"
  lang ruby
  file positional.rb
  expect VYQL-RF-002
  code
  ```
  class AuthorizationsController < ApplicationController
    def create
      redirect_to params[:redirect_uri]
    end
  end
  ```

test "rank1084: the same redirect target passed as a keyword argument"
  lang ruby
  file keyword.rb
  reject VYQL-RF-002
  code
  ```
  class AuthorizationsController < ApplicationController
    def create
      redirect_to location: params[:redirect_uri]
    end
  end
  ```

# Taint reaches a redirect through object construction and an attribute read,
# so the object hop in the real code is not what stops it.

test "rank1084: taint reaches a redirect through a constructed error object"
  lang ruby
  file object_hop.rb
  expect VYQL-RF-002
  code
  ```
  class AuthorizationsController < ApplicationController
    def create
      error = ErrorResponse.new(name: :invalid_request, redirect_uri: params[:redirect_uri])
      redirect_to error.redirect_uri
    end
  end
  ```
